        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was Judge Ritter born?\n\nAnswer A: July 14, 1868\n  about: Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter (July 14, 1868 – October 15, 1951) was an American lawyer, judge, and member of the Republican political party.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: January 1, 1900\n  about: Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter (January 1, 1900 – October 15, 1951) was an American lawyer, judge, and member of the Republican political party.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter (July 14, 1868 – October 15, 1951) was an American lawyer, judge, and member of the Republican political party. He served in the United States District Court for the Southern District of Florida but was impeached and removed from office, only the fourth official to be removed. Ritter was born in Indianapolis, Indiana, in 1868. His sister was Mary Ritter Beard, the wife of Charles A. Beard; both were noted historians. Halsted Ritter attended DePauw University, receiving his Bachelor of Philosophy in 1891, LL.B. in 1892, and Master of Arts in 1893. Ritter was\nDocument 2: Halsted L. Ritter Halsted Lockwood Ritter (January 1, 1900 – October 15, 1951) was an American lawyer, judge, and member of the Republican political party. He served in the United States District Court for the Southern District of Florida but was impeached and removed from office, only the fourth official to be removed. Ritter was born in Indianapolis, Indiana, in 1900. His sister was Mary Ritter Beard, the wife of Charles A. Beard; both were noted historians. Halsted Ritter attended DePauw University, receiving his Bachelor of Philosophy in 1921, LL.B. in 1922, and Master of Arts in 1923. Ritter was\nDocument 3: Willis William Ritter Willis William Ritter (January 24, 1899 – March 4, 1978) was a United States federal judge. Ritter was born in Salt Lake City, Utah, and grew up in Midway and Park City. He received an A.B. from the University of Utah, and an LL.B. from the University of Chicago Law School in 1924. He was in private practice in Chicago, Illinois, and Washington, D.C. from 1924 to 1926. He was a professor of law at the University of Utah from 1926 to 1950, and in private practice in Salt Lake City, Utah from 1935 to 1949, also\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
